# Sein Pan Electronic — Internationalization (i18n) Implementation Guide

**Languages:** English (`en`) · Myanmar / Burmese (`my`) · Japanese (`ja`)
**Stack:** React + **Vite** + Tailwind CSS
**Companion to:** *Technical Blueprint* and *Frontend UI/UX Guide*

> **Stack note:** This guide targets your **Vite** setup (client-rendered SPA). That changes the recommended library vs. a Next.js app — Vite has no server, so we use the client-side `react-i18next` stack with browser language detection. If you later move the storefront to Next.js App Router, switch to `next-intl` (server-component aware); the translation JSON files you write here carry over unchanged. A short Next.js migration note is at the end.

---

## 1. Framework Recommendation

Use **`react-i18next`** — it is the standard for React SPAs, framework-agnostic, and fits Vite perfectly.

| Package | Role |
|---|---|
| `i18next` | Core engine (interpolation, plurals, fallback). |
| `react-i18next` | React bindings — the `useTranslation` hook, `<Trans>` component. |
| `i18next-browser-languagedetector` | Auto-detects + **persists** language (localStorage → cookie → browser). |
| `i18next-http-backend` *(optional)* | Lazy-loads JSON per language over HTTP instead of bundling all upfront. |

**Why this over alternatives:**
- **vs. `react-intl` (FormatJS):** react-i18next has simpler key-based JSON, built-in detection/persistence, and namespaces — less boilerplate for a content site.
- **vs. rolling your own Context:** you'd reinvent pluralization, interpolation, fallback chains, and lazy loading. Not worth it.
- **For < ~3 languages and a modest catalog**, bundle translations directly (skip `http-backend`). Add lazy loading only when JSON files get large.

```bash
npm install i18next react-i18next i18next-browser-languagedetector
# optional, for lazy loading:
# npm install i18next-http-backend
```

---

## 2. Translation File Structure

Use **namespaces** — one folder per language, split by feature so files stay small and teams don't collide. Place them in `src/locales` (bundled) or `public/locales` (if lazy-loading via http-backend).

```
src/locales/
├─ en/
│  ├─ common.json      # nav, buttons, footer — shared everywhere
│  ├─ home.json        # hero, integration grid, bento sections
│  └─ catalog.json     # product/filter strings
├─ my/
│  ├─ common.json
│  ├─ home.json
│  └─ catalog.json
└─ ja/
   ├─ common.json
   ├─ home.json
   └─ catalog.json
```

**`src/locales/en/common.json`**
```json
{
  "brand": "Sein Pan Electronic",
  "since": "Since 1989",
  "nav": { "catalog": "Catalog", "about": "About", "contact": "Contact" },
  "cta": { "explore": "Explore catalog", "learnMore": "Learn more", "quote": "Request a quote" },
  "language": "Language"
}
```

**`src/locales/en/home.json`**
```json
{
  "integrations": {
    "label": "Integrations",
    "title": "Connect with your <1>favorite components</1>",
    "subtitle": "Browse parts, tools, and modules — trusted by Myanmar's makers since 1989."
  },
  "insights": {
    "title": "Customer insights",
    "cards": {
      "conversion": { "title": "Conversion tracking", "body": "Track the journey from first click to final sale." },
      "analytics":  { "title": "Real-time analytics", "body": "See clicks, leads, and sales in full detail, live." },
      "insights":   { "title": "Customer insights",   "body": "Understand journey, lifetime value, and retention." }
    }
  }
}
```

**`src/locales/my/common.json`** (Burmese)
```json
{
  "brand": "စိန်ပန်း အီလက်ထရွန်းနစ်",
  "since": "၁၉၈၉ ခုနှစ်မှစ၍",
  "nav": { "catalog": "ပစ္စည်းစာရင်း", "about": "အကြောင်း", "contact": "ဆက်သွယ်ရန်" },
  "cta": { "explore": "ပစ္စည်းများ ကြည့်ရှုရန်", "learnMore": "ပိုမိုလေ့လာရန်", "quote": "စျေးနှုန်း တောင်းခံရန်" },
  "language": "ဘာသာစကား"
}
```

**`src/locales/ja/common.json`** (Japanese)
```json
{
  "brand": "セインパン・エレクトロニック",
  "since": "1989年創業",
  "nav": { "catalog": "カタログ", "about": "会社概要", "contact": "お問い合わせ" },
  "cta": { "explore": "カタログを見る", "learnMore": "詳しく見る", "quote": "見積もりを依頼" },
  "language": "言語"
}
```

> **Key-naming rules:** nest by feature (`cta.explore`), keep keys in **English** (never translate the key itself), and keep the **same key set across all three files** — a missing key falls back to `en` but should be caught in review (Section 6.3).

---

## 3. The `i18n.js` Configuration File

Create `src/i18n.js` and import it once at app entry (before `<App/>` renders).

```js
// src/i18n.js
import i18n from "i18next";
import { initReactI18next } from "react-i18next";
import LanguageDetector from "i18next-browser-languagedetector";

// Bundle translations (small app). For large apps, swap to i18next-http-backend.
import enCommon from "./locales/en/common.json";
import enHome from "./locales/en/home.json";
import enCatalog from "./locales/en/catalog.json";
import myCommon from "./locales/my/common.json";
import myHome from "./locales/my/home.json";
import myCatalog from "./locales/my/catalog.json";
import jaCommon from "./locales/ja/common.json";
import jaHome from "./locales/ja/home.json";
import jaCatalog from "./locales/ja/catalog.json";

export const SUPPORTED = [
  { code: "en", label: "English",  native: "English",   dir: "ltr" },
  { code: "my", label: "Myanmar",  native: "မြန်မာ",     dir: "ltr" },
  { code: "ja", label: "Japanese", native: "日本語",      dir: "ltr" },
];

const resources = {
  en: { common: enCommon, home: enHome, catalog: enCatalog },
  my: { common: myCommon, home: myHome, catalog: myCatalog },
  ja: { common: jaCommon, home: jaHome, catalog: jaCatalog },
};

i18n
  .use(LanguageDetector)      // detect + persist
  .use(initReactI18next)      // wire into React
  .init({
    resources,
    fallbackLng: "en",
    supportedLngs: ["en", "my", "ja"],
    ns: ["common", "home", "catalog"],
    defaultNS: "common",
    interpolation: { escapeValue: false }, // React already escapes
    detection: {
      // order of where to look for a saved/preferred language
      order: ["localStorage", "cookie", "navigator", "htmlTag"],
      caches: ["localStorage", "cookie"],   // <-- persistence happens here
      lookupLocalStorage: "sp_lang",
      lookupCookie: "sp_lang",
    },
    react: { useSuspense: false }, // simpler for a bundled SPA
  });

// Keep <html lang> + font class in sync on every change (Section 5)
i18n.on("languageChanged", (lng) => {
  const meta = SUPPORTED.find((l) => l.code === lng) || SUPPORTED[0];
  document.documentElement.lang = lng;
  document.documentElement.dir = meta.dir;
  document.documentElement.dataset.lang = lng; // CSS hook for fonts
});

export default i18n;
```

Wire it in at the entry point:

```jsx
// src/main.jsx
import React from "react";
import ReactDOM from "react-dom/client";
import App from "./App";
import "./i18n";          // <-- import BEFORE rendering
import "./index.css";

ReactDOM.createRoot(document.getElementById("root")).render(
  <React.StrictMode>
    <App />
  </React.StrictMode>,
);
```

**Persistence is automatic.** Because `caches: ["localStorage", "cookie"]` is set, the moment a user switches language, i18next writes `sp_lang` to `localStorage`. On the next visit, `detection.order` finds it first and restores it — no extra code. Cookie is a fallback for environments where localStorage is blocked.

---

## 4. The Language Switcher Component

One switcher, used in **both** the Hub (compact nav) and Immersive (full-screen) pages — driven by a `variant` prop so it stays visually consistent with your copper/PCB theme.

```jsx
// src/components/i18n/LanguageSwitcher.jsx
import { useTranslation } from "react-i18next";
import { useState, useRef, useEffect } from "react";
import { SUPPORTED } from "../../i18n";

export function LanguageSwitcher({ variant = "hub" }) {
  const { i18n, t } = useTranslation("common");
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  const ref = useRef(null);
  const current = SUPPORTED.find((l) => l.code === i18n.language) || SUPPORTED[0];

  // close on outside click
  useEffect(() => {
    const onClick = (e) => ref.current && !ref.current.contains(e.target) && setOpen(false);
    document.addEventListener("mousedown", onClick);
    return () => document.removeEventListener("mousedown", onClick);
  }, []);

  const change = (code) => {
    i18n.changeLanguage(code); // persists + updates <html lang> automatically
    setOpen(false);
  };

  return (
    <div className="relative" ref={ref}>
      <button
        onClick={() => setOpen((o) => !o)}
        aria-label={t("language")}
        aria-expanded={open}
        className={[
          "inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-full border border-line",
          "text-sm text-slate-200 transition hover:border-copper-500/50 hover:text-copper-300",
          variant === "immersive" ? "px-4 py-2.5 bg-white/[0.04] backdrop-blur" : "px-3 py-1.5",
        ].join(" ")}
      >
        <span aria-hidden>🌐</span>
        <span>{current.native}</span>
        <span className="text-xs opacity-70">▾</span>
      </button>

      {open && (
        <ul
          role="listbox"
          className="absolute right-0 z-50 mt-2 w-44 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-white/10
                     bg-ink-800/90 backdrop-blur-xl shadow-[0_20px_50px_-20px_rgba(0,0,0,0.7)]"
        >
          {SUPPORTED.map((l) => (
            <li key={l.code}>
              <button
                role="option"
                aria-selected={l.code === current.code}
                onClick={() => change(l.code)}
                className={[
                  "flex w-full items-center justify-between px-4 py-2.5 text-sm transition",
                  l.code === current.code
                    ? "text-copper-300 bg-copper-500/10"
                    : "text-slate-300 hover:bg-white/[0.04] hover:text-copper-300",
                ].join(" ")}
              >
                <span>{l.native}</span>
                <span className="text-xs text-slate-500">{l.label}</span>
              </button>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      )}
    </div>
  );
}
```

Usage:
```jsx
<LanguageSwitcher variant="hub" />        {/* compact, in the header nav */}
<LanguageSwitcher variant="immersive" />  {/* glassy, on full-screen pages */}
```

Because it reads `i18n.language` from context, **every** switcher instance updates simultaneously — the Hub and Immersive pages never drift out of sync.

---

## 5. Font Handling for Myanmar & Japanese (the critical step)

This is what separates a professional multilingual site from a broken one. Latin fonts **cannot** render Burmese or Japanese glyphs — you must load script-specific fonts and switch them by language. The `data-lang` attribute set in `i18n.js` is the CSS hook.

**5.1 Load the fonts** (Google Fonts in `index.html`, or self-host for performance):
```html
<!-- index.html <head> -->
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
<link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
<link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Inter:wght@400;500;600&family=Noto+Sans+Myanmar:wght@400;500;600&family=Noto+Sans+JP:wght@400;500;700&display=swap" rel="stylesheet" />
```

**5.2 Switch font by language** (`src/index.css`, layered with Tailwind):
```css
:root { --font-app: "Inter", system-ui, sans-serif; }

html[data-lang="my"] { --font-app: "Noto Sans Myanmar", "Inter", sans-serif; }
html[data-lang="ja"] { --font-app: "Noto Sans JP", "Inter", sans-serif; }

body { font-family: var(--font-app); }

/* Burmese needs more line-height — stacked diacritics get clipped otherwise */
html[data-lang="my"] { line-height: 1.85; }
```

**5.3 Wire the variable into Tailwind** so `font-sans` follows the language:
```ts
// tailwind.config.ts
theme: { extend: { fontFamily: { sans: ["var(--font-app)", "system-ui", "sans-serif"] } } }
```

Now switching language automatically swaps the entire UI font — no component changes needed.

> **Burmese-specific gotchas:** (1) use **Unicode**, not legacy Zawgyi encoding — Noto Sans Myanmar is Unicode-only; (2) give Burmese extra `line-height` (done above) so stacked marks aren't clipped; (3) avoid `text-transform: uppercase` on Burmese — it has no case and can corrupt rendering. Scope any `uppercase` utility to `html[data-lang="en"]`.

---

## 6. Applying i18n to Existing Components (without breaking the UI)

The golden rule: **i18n changes the text source, not the markup or classes.** Your Tailwind classes, layout, and animations are untouched — you only swap hard-coded strings for `t("key")`.

### 6.1 `BentoCard` — pass translation keys, not strings

The component stays identical; the *parent* resolves keys. Two clean patterns:

**Pattern A — parent translates (simplest, keeps BentoCard dumb):**
```jsx
// home page
import { useTranslation } from "react-i18next";
import { BentoCard } from "../components/bento/BentoCard";

export function InsightsSection() {
  const { t } = useTranslation("home");
  const cards = ["conversion", "analytics", "insights"];

  return (
    <BentoGrid>
      <BentoCard className="md:row-span-2" accent><ProfileSummary /></BentoCard>
      {cards.map((k) => (
        <BentoCard
          key={k}
          title={t(`insights.cards.${k}.title`)}
          body={t(`insights.cards.${k}.body`)}
          highlight={k === "insights"}
          href="#"
        />
      ))}
    </BentoGrid>
  );
}
```

**Pattern B — BentoCard accepts an optional `tKey` and translates itself** (handy when used in many places):
```jsx
// BentoCard.jsx — add a few lines, no design change
import { useTranslation } from "react-i18next";

export function BentoCard({ tKey, title, body, ns = "home", ...props }) {
  const { t } = useTranslation(ns);
  const _title = tKey ? t(`${tKey}.title`) : title;   // fallback to raw prop
  const _body  = tKey ? t(`${tKey}.body`)  : body;
  // ...rest of component unchanged, render {_title} / {_body}
}

// usage: <BentoCard tKey="insights.cards.analytics" highlight href="#" />
```

Pattern B is backward-compatible — existing `title="..."` usages still work, so nothing breaks during migration.

### 6.2 `IntegrationGrid` — translate the tile labels

Keep `name` as a **key**, resolve at render:
```jsx
import { useTranslation } from "react-i18next";

const tools = [
  { key: "tools.mcu",        icon: "/icons/mcu.svg",       col: 1, row: 1 },
  { key: "tools.resistors",  icon: "/icons/resistor.svg",  col: 3, row: 1 },
  // ...
];

export function IntegrationGrid() {
  const { t } = useTranslation("home");
  return (
    <div className="grid grid-cols-6 gap-4 auto-rows-[88px]">
      {tools.map((tool, i) => (
        <motion.button /* ...all existing motion/className unchanged... */ key={tool.key}>
          <img src={tool.icon} alt={t(tool.key)} className="h-8 w-8" />
          <span className="sp-name">{t(tool.key)}</span>
        </motion.button>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```

For the heading with an inline copper highlight, use the `<Trans>` component so the `<1>...</1>` tag in the JSON maps to your styled span:
```jsx
import { Trans } from "react-i18next";

<h2 className="text-4xl font-semibold text-white">
  <Trans i18nKey="integrations.title" ns="home">
    Connect with your <span className="text-copper-400">favorite components</span>
  </Trans>
</h2>
```

### 6.3 Reusable convenience hook

`react-i18next` already gives you `useTranslation`. Add a thin wrapper only if you want app defaults (e.g. always load `common` + a feature namespace):

```js
// src/hooks/useT.js
import { useTranslation } from "react-i18next";

// useT("home") -> t() reads home first, common as fallback
export function useT(ns = "common") {
  return useTranslation([ns, "common"]);
}
```
```jsx
const { t } = useT("home");
t("insights.title");      // from home.json
t("cta.explore");         // falls back to common.json
```

### 6.4 Guardrails so translations never silently break the UI

- **Missing-key visibility (dev only):** `saveMissing: true` + a `missingKeyHandler` that `console.warn`s — surfaces untranslated keys during development.
- **Layout resilience:** German/Japanese strings can be much longer or denser than English. Avoid fixed-width buttons; use `min-w` + padding and `truncate`/`line-clamp` where overflow is possible. Test each card at all three languages.
- **No keys in the JSX tree** other than via `t()`/`<Trans>` — keeps a clean separation so a translator can edit JSON without touching React.
- **CI check:** a tiny script that diffs the key sets of `en`, `my`, `ja` and fails the build on mismatch keeps the three files in lockstep.

---

## 7. Migration Note — if you move to Next.js later

If the storefront moves to Next.js App Router (per the Blueprint), switch the *library* to **`next-intl`** (server-component-aware, locale-routing via `/en`, `/my`, `/ja`). Your `*.json` translation files transfer unchanged — only the config and the hook import change (`useTranslations` from `next-intl`). The `LanguageSwitcher`, font CSS, and key structure all carry over.

---

## Setup Checklist

1. `npm i i18next react-i18next i18next-browser-languagedetector`
2. Create `src/locales/{en,my,ja}/{common,home,catalog}.json`.
3. Add `src/i18n.js` (Section 3) and import it in `main.jsx`.
4. Load Noto fonts + add the `data-lang` font CSS (Section 5).
5. Drop `<LanguageSwitcher/>` into the header and immersive layout.
6. Migrate `BentoCard` / `IntegrationGrid` strings to `t()` keys (Section 6).
7. Verify: switch languages → fonts swap, text persists across reload, no layout breakage.

**The one principle:** internationalization touches *content*, not *design*. Every Tailwind class, animation, and the copper/PCB theme stays exactly as-is — you're only swapping where the text comes from, plus loading the right font per script.

